  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAn expansive area of high pressure dominates the weather \npattern from the Upper Midwest into the Northeast through the \nupcoming week supporting a prolonged period of dry and mainly \nsunny weather. Temperatures remain seasonable as well. Daytime \nhighs each day through Thursday rise into the upper 50s to low \nto mid 60s and favorable radiational cooling most nights \nsupports lows in the low to mid 30s. Persistent northerly winds\nwill also maintain consistent dry air advection with relative\nhumidity values dropping to 30 to 45% each afternoon through\nWednesday. While winds today will remain weak generally under \n5kts, northerly winds tomorrow become a bit breezy as the\npressure gradient over the Northeast tightens in response to \nhigh pressure strengthening to our north and a nor'easter\ndeveloping well off shore. This should support sustained northerly\nwinds ranging 5-10kts and gusts up to 20-25kts, strongest during\npeak heating hours in the north-south oriented Hudson Valley. \nDespite winds becoming stronger, RH values should remain just \nhigh enough to mitigate fire spread concerns.\n\nLatest ensembles and cluster guidance continues to keep the\ndeveloping nor'easter positioned well to our south and east off\nthe mid-Atlanatic/Carolina coast through the middle to end of\nthe week. As it slowly exits further out of sea Wednesday into \nThursday, there is enough ensemble support to suggest that it \ntracks close enough to the coast to result in breezy winds. The \nlatest probabilistic guidance from the NBM show a 25 to 50% \nchance for wind gusts to exceed 25mph in the 24hr period between\n2am Thurs and 2am Fri with up to a 50 - 75% chance in the mid- \nHudson Valley into Litchfield County, CT. However, the \nprobability for gusts exceeding 30mph is lower mainly under 30%.\nThere is a strong consensus to keep the nor'easter far enough \noff shore to prevent any rain from reaching eastern NY/western \nNew England. While the latest NHC forecast shows a 40% chance \nfor this to become a subtropical cyclone as it exits out to sea\nduring the middle to end of the week, we do not anticipate any \nsignificant impacts in eastern NY/western New England given how far \nout to sea it is.",
